## ABSTRACT
To date, the effective discrimination of anionic sulfonate surfactants with tiny differences in structure, considered as environmentally noxious xenobiotics, is still a challenge for traditional analytical techniques. Fortunately, sensor array becomes the best choice for recognizing targets with similar structures or physical/chemical properties by virtue of principal component analysis (PCA, a statistical technique). Herein, benefiting from the construction of the statistical strategy and using two types of luminescent metal-organic frameworks (LMOFs, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88) as sensing elements, high-throughput discrimination and detection of five anionic sulfonate surfactants as well as their mixtures are nicely realized for the first time. Significantly, the stacking interaction of aromatic rings and dynamic quenching play essential roles in the generation of diverse fluorescence responses and unique fingerprint maps for individual anionic sulfonate surfactants. Moreover, the mixtures of anionic sulfonate surfactants are also satisfactorily distinguished in environmental water samples, demonstrating the practicability of the sensor array. On the basis of the PCA method, this strategy converts general fluorescence signals into unique optical fingerprints of individual analytes, providing a new opportunity for the application of LMOFs in the field of analytes recognition.
KEYWORDS: metal-organic frameworks; sensor array; statistical technique; anionic sulfonate surfactants; discrimination
## INTRODUCTION
Colorimetric method and fluorometry, as traditional analytical techniques, play pivotal roles in the field of analytical chemistry because of their easily interpreted outputs, conveniently operated instruments, and sensitive rapid responses. In an ideal colorimetric or fluorescent-based sensing system, each sensor is engineered for one given analyte. However, the selectivity of this sensing system is sometimes impossible to realize, hindering its practical application in realizing multiple discrimination of similar analytes. For example, Lu et al. achieved the sensitive detection of bio−thiols (cysteine, homocysteine, glutathione) via reductive carbon nitride nanodots, while it was inaccessible to distinguish cysteine/homocysteine/glutathione from each other in complex biological fluids. Moreover, a quenching fluorescence sensor based on molybdenum disulfide nanoplates was used to sensitively detect tetracyclines but failed in selectivity. Considering the practical requirements, an array-based sensing technique, called "sensor array" or "chemical nose/tongue", is emerging like mushrooms in analyte recognition. Sensor arrays mimic the mammalian olfactory or gustatory systems and emerge in the early chemometric world. Traditional "lock-key" sensing mode requires specific recognition or receptors, while array-based sensing was designed as libraries of cross-reactive sensing units that behave differentially to analytes. The collective responses from these libraries form a distinctive "fingerprint" for each target, so a range of analogs can be identified through linear discriminant analysis (LDA) or principal component analysis (PCA). Therefore, sensor array enables the high-throughput differentiation of targets with an exceedingly small
discrepancy in theory. Several outstanding examples have been published to recognize six thiols with urease-metal ion pairs,\(^{7}\) seventeen metal ions with protein/lanthanide complex,\(^{8}\) five phosphates with graphitic carbon nitride nanosheets and \(o\)-phenylenediamine,\(^{9}\) and diverse mammalian cell types with conjugated polymers and green fluorescent protein.\(^{10}\) It is worth mentioning that the chiral enantiomer mixtures of \(L\)-cysteine and \(D\)-cysteine can be entirely distinguished by PCA, emphasizing the capacity of this chiral identification in biofluids.\(^{11,12}\)
In the course of this research, the anionic surfactants as a species of the critical environmental pollutant have attracted our attention. Anionic surfactants are usually presented as inexpensive active constituents and emulsifiers in personal cleaning agents, domestic detergents, and industrial solvents.\(^{13,14}\) Due to the extensive use of anionic surfactants, they are frequently encountered in domestic and industrial wastewaters and eventually accumulate in the environment, bringing out a lot of environmental problems. Once exposed to a high level of surfactants, the skin and eyes of humans will be irritated.\(^{15}\) Sodium dodecylbenzenesulphonate (SDBS), as a typical anionic surfactant, has been quantitatively detected by capillary electrophoresis,\(^{16}\) potentiometry,\(^{17}\) and optical detection.\(^{18-21}\) For instance, Li and co-workers chose amphiphilic naphthalimide derivatives as probes and employed the changes in color and optical properties to detect anionic surfactants.\(^{20}\) Same like this, most of the published articles in optical detection are inaccessible to distinguish sodium dodecyl sulfonate (SDSO), sodium alpha-olefin sulfonate (AOS), sodium cocoyl isethionate (SCI), and methyl ester sulfonate (MES), which are similar to the structure of SDBS and belong to anionic sulfonate surfactants
(Figure S1). Apart from the attempts in considering new ways to diminish their environmental damage, another significant aspect is the exploitation of novel and improved methods to detect them. Fortunately, the emergence of sensor arrays promises that similar structures or chemical/physical properties can be perceived and recognized simultaneously. Up to date, no literature has been devoted to differentiating anionic sulfonate surfactants via sensor arrays.
Inspired by luminescent metal–organic frameworks (LMOFs), a unique optical sensor with periodic network structure, we envisage that LMOFs are favorable alternatives to sensor arrays for their facile synthesis conditions, excellent luminescence features, and durable sensing features.\[22-24\] In the preliminary experiment, we found that SDBS can dramatically enhance the fluorescence intensities of the two LOMFs (NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88), while SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES can inhibit their fluorescence. Therefore, only SDBS can be distinguished from anionic sulfonate surfactants via single fluorometry. Enlightened by the sensor array, herein, we demonstrate that the structurally stable LOMFs (NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88) can be modulated by various anionic sulfonate surfactants, constituting the basis of the fabricated sensor arrays for anionic sulfonate surfactants differentiation. From Scheme 1, two recognition elements, NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88, were selected to establish the sensor arrays for the purpose of anionic sulfonate surfactants recognition. Subsequently, five anionic sulfonate surfactants, including SDBS, SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES, were added to interact with two receptors, respectively. Because of slight differences in the structures of the five anionic sulfonate surfactants, the "fingerprints"
generated by the diverse fluorescence intensities were collected and analyzed by the PCA method. Accordingly, taking advantage of the combination of the array-based fluorescence signals and the statistical process, the active discrimination and detection of anionic sulfonate surfactants were successfully performed. More importantly, two recognition elements were also successfully used to identify the mixtures of five anionic sulfonate surfactants in the reservoir. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first paper on the identification of anionic sulfonate surfactants, which extends the application of LMOFs in the identification of analogs.
