## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Reagents. Sodium dodecylbenzenesulphonate (SDBS), sodium dodecyl sulfonate (SDSO), sodium alpha-olefin sulfonate (AOS), sodium cocoyl isethionate (SCI),
methyl ester sulfonate (MES), iron (III) chloride hexahydrate \(\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\), nitric acid \(\left(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\right)\), and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) were obtained from Chengdu Kelong Chemical Reagent Plant (Sichuan, China). Zirconium chloride \((\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},\ \geq98\%)\), 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC), dimethylformamide (DMF), and ethanol (EtOH) were purchased from Adamas Reagent, Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All chemicals were used without further purification, and ultrapure water (18.2 MΩ cm) was used throughout the whole experiment.
Apparatus. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were carried out on a D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer (Bruker, Germany). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of the samples were performed by a Bruker IFS 113v spectrometer (Bruker, Germany) after pelleting the fine powder with KBr. An Escalab 250Xi X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) was used to perform X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. Fluorescence measurements were carried out on a TECAN microplate reader (Infinite M200 Pro, Switzerland) with a black 96-well microplate. The fluorescence spectra were recorded on a Hitachi F-4500 spectrophotometer (Tokyo, Japan). The morphologies of materials were observed on a scanning electron microscope (SEM, Zeiss Merlin compact, Germany).
Preparation of \(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88. \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 was synthesized according to the previous literature with slight modification. Typically, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 was synthesized by mixing \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC, and DMF with a molar ratio of 1:1:750 at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). After cooling to room temperature, the resulting solid was repeatedly washed with DMF and EtOH and dried overnight at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum.
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 was prepared as published articles.\(^{26,27}\) In brief, \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.3740 g) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC (0.2500 g) were dissolved in \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF with ultrasound for about \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). Next, the mixture was transferred into a Teflon liner autoclave and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The formed brown precipitate was collected, washed with DMF and EtOH three times, and dried in vacuum at \(60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight.
Array-Based Sensing Procedure. In a black 96-well plate, two LMOFs (\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88; \(2\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\), \(125\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) were separately pipetted into the 5 columns (for five surfactants) and 5 rows (5 replicates). Next, the desired concentration of anionic sulfonate surfactants was added into their corresponding columns, and these mixtures were diluted to \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) with \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}/\mathrm{NaOH}\) solution (pH 5.0, 1 M). Then, the above solutions were equilibrated for \(50\ \mathrm{min}\) at room temperature. Finally, the fluorescence intensity (\(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=320\ \mathrm{nm}\), \(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=430\ \mathrm{nm}\)) of mixtures were recorded by a microplate reader. As a consequence, five kinds of anionic sulfonate surfactants were measured against the two LMOFs five times to provide a training-data matrix of 2 arrays \(\times\ 5\) surfactants \(\times\ 5\) replicates.
Data Analysis. The change of fluorescence, defined as \(F/F_{0}\), where \(F_{0}\) and \(F\) are the fluorescence intensity of \(430\ \mathrm{nm}\) in the absence and presence of anionic sulfonate surfactants, was utilized to measure the effect of five anionic sulfonate surfactants on the fluorescence of the two receptors. The ratio \(F/F_{0}\) was employed as the response signal for array sensing analysis and dealt with principal component analysis (PCA) in SPSS 24.0 software (IBM). The data plotting was handled with GraphPad Prism 8.0 software.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Design of LMOFs-Based Pattern Discrimination Assay. In this article, two classes of LMOFs (NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88) with different valence states of metal ions (i.e., Zr\(^{4+}\) and Fe\(^{3+}\)) were chosen to effectively develop diverse receptors. NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88 were obtained by a facile solvothermal method. The crystallinity of the two as-synthesized LMOFs was verified by PXRD. As shown in Figure 1A and 1C, the PXRD patterns of synthesized NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88 are well consistent with the simulated one, respectively, demonstrating that two LMOFs were successfully synthesized. Figure 1B and 1D shows the SEM images of the two LMOFs, where typical octahedral morphologies are observed. The average size of the NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88 is about 200 and 250 nm, respectively. A comparison of the functional groups of NH\(_2\)-UiO-66, NH\(_2\)-MIL-88, and the ligand NH\(_2\)-BDC was investigated by the FTIR spectrometer (Figure S2). The strong band at the range of 2500−3300 cm\(^{-1}\) was O−H wagging vibrations of carboxyl groups in the ligand NH\(_2\)-BDC. Whereas, for the spectra of NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88, such a broad band vanishes, confirming that NH\(_2\)-BDC is linked into the framework of the two LMOFs with full deprotonations.\(^{28,29}\) The characteristic bands in the region of 3250−3500 cm\(^{-1}\) are ascribed to N−H stretching vibration (marked with dotted box in Figure S2) of NH\(_2\) groups.\(^{30}\) Meanwhile, the preserved but comparatively weaker peaks at 1257 and 1338 cm\(^{-1}\) (labeled with stars in Figure S2) are assigned to the characteristic C−N stretching vibration, turning out the presence of aromatic amines.\(^{31}\) The new peak of Zr−O is revealed at 600−800 cm\(^{-1}\),\(^{32}\) while the Fe−O vibration is observed at 524 cm\(^{-1}\) (marked
with dashed circles in curves b and c), suggesting the coordination between \( \mathrm{Zr}^{4+} \) or \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) and O atoms in \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-BDC.\(^{31,32}\) In addition, the chemical compositions of two LMOFs were confirmed by XPS. The peaks of C (1s), N (1s), O (1s), and Zr (3d) all appear on the survey spectrum of \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-UiO-66 (Figure S3A). The high-resolution spectra of O 1s and Zr 3d are displayed in Figure S3B and S3C, respectively. The O 1s spectrum is deconvoluted into three peaks at 529.6, 531.5, and 532.8 eV, which are attributed to the binding energies of Zr–O–Zr, Zr–OH, and O–H, respectively.\(^{33}\) Two peaks at 182.45 and 184.81 eV, in the Zr 3d spectrum, are ascribed to the binding energies for Zr \( 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2} \) and Zr \( 3\mathrm{d}_{3/2} \) coordinated with the carboxylic groups in the ligand \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-BDC.\(^{34}\) Similarly, the survey spectrum, O 1s and Fe 2p spectra of \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-MIL-88 are given in Figure S3D-F, respectively. The elements of C (1s), N (1s), O (1s), and Fe (2p) are able to be observed in the survey spectrum. The high-resolution O 1s exhibits that the components of Fe–O, C=O, and –OH exist in the \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-MIL-88.\(^{35}\) According to Wu' group,\(^{36}\) the binding energies located at 712.0 and 724.5 eV are assigned to the Fe \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) and Fe \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \), and the peak at 717.9 eV is the satellite signal of oxidized iron. The above results indicated the successful synthesis of \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-UiO-66 and \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-MIL-88.
Herein, we employ \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 to design an LMOFs-based sensor array for high-throughput discrimination of anionic sulfonate surfactants. The sensing principle depends on the diverse emission profiles of the two LMOFs with an excitation wavelength of \(330\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2A). Their fluorescence characteristics are different in intensity, demonstrating the contribution of ligand-to-metal charge transfer processes between various transition metals and the ligand is different. Upon exposed with anionic sulfonate surfactants, the selected sensor units exhibit various fluorescence changes and supply apparent responses to five anionic sulfonate surfactants to achieve the goal of pattern discrimination. From Figure 2B and 2C, SDBS is available to enhance the fluorescence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88, while a "turn-off" response can be noted after adding SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES (each at \(4\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)). The
individual fluorescence signal only distinguishes SDBS but fails in identifying the other four anionic sulfonate surfactants. Interestingly, PCA, a statistical procedure lies in calculating orthogonal eigenvectors (principal components, PCs) in the direction of the maximum variance to convert a set of possibly correlating variables into a set of linearly uncorrelated data and reduce the dimensionality of a data set for easier interpretation.\(^{38,39}\) Combining the unique fluorescence responses and the PCA method, a novel sensory platform for detection and differentiation of five anionic sulfonate surfactants is successfully fabricated.
Discrimination of Anionic Sulfonate Surfactants. To estimate the discrimination ability of LMOFs sensor array we established, five anionic sulfonate surfactants, including SDBS, SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES, were selected as targets. In Figure 3A, each fluorescence response for a given anionic sulfonate surfactant is separately evaluated by the two LMOFs sensors (\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88), and relative fluorescence changes were recorded for producing pattern \(F/F_{0}\) data. A heat map is another visualization tool for comparing data subset responses.\(^{40}\) In the heat map, each data point is expressed in a color, allowing the readers to intuitively see differences and
similarities in the analyte responses.\(^{41}\) From Figure 3B, the corresponding heat map was obtained by the GraphPad Prism 8.0 software, making the results more intuitive. The resultant fluorescence response patterns (\(F/F_{0}\) data set) are subjected to PCA to transform the training matrix (2 arrays \(\times\) 5 surfactants \(\times\) 5 replicates) into "fingerprints" and generate two PCs. As shown in Figure 3C, the first principal component (PC1, 95.105%) and the second principal component (PC2, 4.895%) are used to produce a two-dimensional (2D) plot: The five anionic sulfonate surfactants displayed superior separation without overlap, revealing that PCA is an effective statistical technique for target surfactants identification. Moreover, the five anionic sulfonate surfactants at no less than \(4\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) concentration levels were available to be separated from each other in the 2D canonical score. Apart from PCA, hierarchical-cluster-analysis (HCA), another statistical classification technique based on squared Euclidean distance, is utilized to determine the similarity of anionic sulfonate surfactants. As expected, all of the 25 cases (5 surfactants \(\times\) 5 replicates) are correctly distributed to their respective groups with no misclassification or error (Figure 3D). The results of PCA and HCA suggest that the LMOFs-based sensory platform realized high-throughput discrimination of anionic sulfonate surfactants with high confidence. Compared to the traditional analytical techniques, the superiority of the proposed sensing system combined with statistical processing has a powerful ability to identify the analytes with puny differences in structures. Significantly, the results of multiple parallel experiments ensure the feasibility and accuracy of the sensor array.
To ensure satisfactory discrimination of the five anionic sulfonate surfactants, the optimization of sensing conditions was explored (Figure S4): (A-C) buffer solutions; (D) reaction temperature; (E) incubation time. It can be seen from Figure S4A-C that the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 showed a high sensitivity to detect the five anionic sulfonate surfactants in \(\mathrm{NaOH/HNO}_{3}\) solution (pH 5.0). In addition, the interaction temperature is pivotal for a sensor in practical application. The fluorescence was unstable at high temperature, so \(25\mathrm{{^\circC}}\) was chosen as the optimal reaction temperature (Figure S4D). The
effect of reaction time on anionic sulfonate surfactants (taking SDBS as an example) detection was continuously monitored. Figure S4E clearly illustrates that the reaction between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and SDBS can be completed after \(50\ \mathrm{min}\). Therefore, pH 5.0 (\(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}/\mathrm{NaOH}\) solution), incubation temperature of \(25\mathrm{{^\circC}}\), and a reaction time of \(50\;\mathrm{min}\) are chosen as the optimal discrimination conditions.
Subsequently, the sensitivity of the array platform to specific surfactants with different concentrations was studied (Figure 4 and Figure S5). It is evident that the "fingerprints" in PCA plots for each anionic sulfonate surfactant with diverse concentrations are well separated without cross-influences. The PC1 is utilized to quantify the concentrations of anionic sulfonate surfactants because PC2 does not exceed \(40\%\). The sensing unit is sensitive to measure certain concentrations of these anionic sulfonate surfactants. From Figure 4B and 4D, the PC1 is proportional to the concentrations of SDBS in the ranges of \(0.04{-}4~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\) and \(4{-}16~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\), as well as MES concentrations in the range of \(0.02{-}6~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\). In addition, the linear range of SDSO is \(0.2{-}10~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\), AOS is \(0.02{-}8~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\), and SCI is \(0.04{-}12~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\) (Figure S5). The above linear relationships indicate that the sensitive determination of individual anionic sulfonate surfactants can be realized by the proposed sensor array.
Same as the animals distinguish the taste, the discrimination ability of the LMOFs sensor array about the component difference of complex mixtures is inaccessible to be sacrificed. As proof of this view, \(4\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) mixture of SDBS and SDSO with different molar ratios were measured (SDBS \(100\%\) , SDBS \(80\%+\) SDSO \(20\%\) , SDBS \(60\%+\) SDSO \(40\%\) , SDBS \(50\%+\) SDSO \(50\%\) , SDBS \(40\%+\) SDSO \(60\%\) , SDBS \(20\%+\) SDSO \(80\%\) , and SDSO \(100\%\) ). The achieved fluorescence intensities were subjected to PCA to produce the score plot. In Figure 5A, the "fingerprints" of mixtures are correctly arranged with the order of molar ratios and distinguished from each other in the PCA
patterns. Dendrogram formed by HCA revealed 30 cases (6 mixtures \(\times\) 5 replicates) are assigned to their respective groups (Figure 5B). From the PCA and HCA results, the proposed assay system is potentially suitable for targets of complex composition.
Mechanism of the Sensory Platform. Inspiringly, SDBS exhibits concentration-dependent enhancement influence on the fluorescence of the LMOFs sensory platform, while SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES show concentration-dependent quenching effect. Therefore, the mechanism involved in this paper is well worth discussing. We suspect that the reason for the different responses of LMOFs towards anionic sulfonate surfactants may be related to the structure of these surfactants, although they are similar with each other (Figure S1). For the fluorescence-enhancement of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88, the O atom on sulfonic acid group of SDBS forms hydrogen bond with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) on the ligand \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC, closing the distance between SDBS and LMOFs. Furthermore, SDBS, different from the other four surfactants, possesses a phenyl group in the structure. Hence, a stacking interaction of aromatic rings, resulting from the overlap of the \(\pi\)-electron cloud between the phenyl group of SDBS and the aryl group
of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88, is a considerable possibility in enhancing the fluorescence intensities of the sensing units. The stacking interaction, taking a vital role in the formation of ion-association complexes and in the reaction of biomacromolecules (such as nucleic acids) with dyes or some aromatic drugs,\(^{42,43}\) has an enormous contribution to the interaction of SDBS with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88. In addition, the results of resonance Rayleigh scattering (RRS), which has a high dependence on the volume of particles, further confirm our speculation. Herein, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 was selected to react with five anionic sulfonate surfactants as a representative (Figure S6, see Supporting Information for RRS procedure). When reacting with SDBS, the RRS signal of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 was dramatically enhanced because of the stacking interaction, but its RRS intensity was almost unchanged when the other four species (SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES) at the same concentration were added. The two LMOFs accordingly identify SDBS from the five surfactants. Moreover, the fluorescence quenching mechanism of the two LMOFs by these four surfactants has also been explored. Figure S7A displays that when SDSO, AOS, SCI, or MES was added to \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 solution, no new absorption peak appeared. There is no non-fluorescent ground-state complex, turning out that dynamic quenching mechanism is accountable for the quenching effect.\(^{44}\) In addition, the fluorescence lifetime was used to demonstrate that dynamic quenching is the dominant factor (Figure S7B-F). For dynamic quenching, the fluorescence lifetime of the excited state molecules will decrease when quenchers exist, and \(\tau_{0}/\tau\) will be equal to \(F_{0}/F\) (where \(\tau\) and \(\tau_{0}\) are the fluorescence lifetimes with and without the quencher, respectively), whereas \(\tau_{0}/\tau = 1\) for static quenching.\(^{45,46}\) The
reaction between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 and SCI was selected as a representative of the quenching behavior. In Table S1, the significant decrease in fluorescence lifetime of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 (\(\tau_{0} = 15.72\) ns, \(\tau = 6.38\) ns; \(\tau_{0}/\tau\) is about equal to \(F_{0}/F\)) is obtained, illustrating that the quenching behavior results from a dynamic quenching process. The \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88 fluorescence lifetimes before and after SDSO, AOS, and MES are also listed in Table S1. In general, the dominant quenching mechanism is caused by dynamic association.
Identification of Anionic Sulfonate Surfactants in Real Samples. Distinguishing anionic sulfonate surfactants in real water samples is quite challenging and meaningful, since most of the current methods do not possess enough selectivity when employed in a complicated circumstance. Here, we collected a water sample from the Beibei reservoir in Chongqing, to assess the practical application of the LMOFs sensor array. Prior to measurement, the raw water sample was centrifuged and boiled, and finally filtered with a \(0.2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) filter membrane to purge the suspended impurities. Afterward, reservoir water samples with spiked surfactants were measured by the sensor array and analyzed via PCA to evaluate the qualitative performance of the sensor array in real-water samples. As expected, the fluorescence signals of the sensor array to the five anionic sulfonate surfactants at \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) in water samples are different. We further successfully converted the diverse responses into unique patterns through PCA (Figure 6A), dividing the 5 anionic sulfonate surfactants into 5 distinct clusters without apparent overlap. This finding indicates that the LMOFs-based array has potentials to discriminate the five anionic sulfonate surfactants in real water samples. A typical real
water sample contains more than one analyte, so it is crucial to quantify the mixture of anionic sulfonate surfactants in samples. For this goal, several mixed groups with diverse molar ratios were added to the water sample (SDBS/SDSO = 10:0, 7.5:2.5, 6:4, 2.5:7.5, 0:10, all in \(4\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)). For comparison, three test samples were prepared: (A) \(3.5\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDBS + \(0.5\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDSO, (B) \(2\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDBS + \(2\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDSO, (C) \(0.5\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDBS + \(3.5\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) SDSO. The above mixed groups in a water sample were also identified in the canonical score plot only using the two PCs, and the test samples were observed in the right position of the PCA plot according to their ratios (Figure 6B). For example, sample A appears among the two standard mixtures with SDBS/SDSO molar ratios at 10:0 and 7.5:2.5, demonstrating that the concentration of SDBS was from 3 to \(4\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) and that of SDSO from 0 to \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) in the reservoir water sample. The detailed data summarized in the table (Figure 6B) emphasize the ability of LMOFs sensor array to perform semi-quantitative sensing of anionic sulfonate surfactants mixtures in real water samples. Hence, the fabricated LMOFs-based sensor array is available to realize a qualitative and semi-quantitative detection of anionic sulfonate surfactants in a complex environment with high accuracy.
## CONCLUSION
On the basis of the two LMOFs (NH\(_2\)-UiO-66 and NH\(_2\)-MIL-88) as receptors, a reliable and high-throughput sensor array for pattern recognition of anionic sulfonate surfactants has been proposed for the first time. Intriguingly, we found that the fluorescence intensities of the two LMOFs were greatly enhanced by SDBS, while they were quenched by other anionic sulfonate surfactants (such as SDSO, AOS, SCI, and MES). It is challenging to discriminate five anionic sulfonate surfactants with similar structure through a single fluorescent signal. Hence, taking advantage of the PCA
statistical method and collecting different fluorescence responses, five anionic sulfonate surfactants have been well distinguished and determined via the two sensing units. Remarkably, the fabricated array system can not only exhibit the excellent ability to discriminate surfactants at different concentrations, but also identify the mixture of anionic sulfonate surfactants in a complicated environment. This article provides a statistical strategy converting the general fluorescence signal of LMOFs into "fingerprints" maps to realize the discrimination of five anionic sulfonate surfactants, bringing new opportunities for the application of LMOFs in the analytical chemistry field.
